make

# To run your project, use the command:
make run

# If you added a file to your project add it to the build section in the Makefile

# Always start the line with a tab in Makefile, it is its syntax

## Generator and worker handoff

The generator uses `processes.txt` and, for HPF, `resources.txt`. To use other
paths, run `./process_generator.out PROCESSES_PATH [RESOURCES_PATH]`. RR asks
for a positive quantum and a nonnegative switch delay. Press Enter to use
a switch delay of one tick.

The generator launches `scheduler.out QUEUE_ID ALGORITHM QUANTUM SWITCH_TICKS
RESOURCES_PATH`. Algorithms are 1 (SJF), 2 (RR), and 3 (HPF). Quantum is 0
outside RR. The resource path is empty outside HPF. For HPF, the scheduler can
call `resource_plan_load` and pass the result to its HPF module.

- Start processing at simulation tick 1. The generator sends arrivals in ID
  order, followed by `TICK_DONE` for each tick up to the final arrival. A tick
  with no arrivals has just `TICK_DONE`. After `INPUT_DONE`, there are no more
  batches to wait for. The scheduler should finish its queued jobs before exit.
- At tick t, call `worker_tick` only for the worker assigned `[t-1, t)`.
  Use the ACK to update the PCB before processing events and dispatching again.
  Stop/resume as required by the policy. A stopped worker must be continued
  before sending a tick. Ignore `SIGPIPE` in the scheduler so pipe failures can
  be handled as errors. On a final ACK, close the pipes and reap using `waitpid`.
- The generator owns the queue and clock lifetime. The scheduler should use
  `destroyClk(false)` to detach. Keep workers in the scheduler's process group
  so the generator can clean them up after interruption or scheduler failure.

## Person 2 scheduler core

Each live job has one PCB with its input fields, acknowledged remaining CPU
time and worker handle (PID and pipe descriptors). The scheduler keeps one
running pointer and linked queues for ready and blocked jobs. The first
dispatch starts process.out through worker_start. A preemption stops that
same child with SIGSTOP and waits for confirmation. A later dispatch resumes
it with SIGCONT. On a final worker acknowledgement the scheduler closes the
pipes, waits for normal child exit, records the finish and frees the PCB.

At each clock tick the scheduler accounts for the preceding CPU interval,
applies resource events and completion, receives the entire arrival batch,
then checks preemption and dispatch. The generator sends simultaneous
arrivals in ID order. While the CPU switches, arrivals continue to enter
the ready queue and workers consume no ticks. The next selection happens
when the switch ends. Dispatch from idle is immediate.

SJF scans the ready queue only at dispatch. It compares total runtime,
arrival time and ID in that order. RR appends and removes jobs in constant
time. At quantum expiry it renews a lone job's slice silently. If another
job is ready, the interrupted job goes behind the current tick's arrivals
and ahead of arrivals in subsequent switch ticks.

All policies use the same event logger and performance accumulator.
Waiting time is elapsed time since arrival minus executed CPU time,
including switch and blocked time. Statistics use unrounded finish WTA
values and a running mean and population variance. CPU utilization uses the
absolute final finish tick. The generator accepts empty inputs, so this
implementation emits an empty log and four zero metrics for that case.

## HPF integration

The scheduler uses the hpf_* interface in scheduler_api.h. It registers
arrivals, delivers each acknowledged CPU tick (including the last one),
applies unblock/block events and compares effective priorities. The HPF
module owns resource release-before-capture ordering and inheritance.
An hpf.c beside the Makefile is included automatically. For a different
filename, build with make HPF_SRC=path/to/module.c. Until the teammate's
module is linked, choosing algorithm 3 exits with an explanatory message.

## Linux tests

Run make test from the repository directory with GCC or another compatible
C compiler, GNU Make and Python 3. The suite runs the real generator,
clock, scheduler and child executables. Each run writes to a temporary
directory. Run simulations sequentially because the clock uses key 300.
The two PDF examples compare every log line and all four performance
lines with the checked-in expected files under tests/examples. Edge cases
also compare complete logs and reports against hand-specified timelines.
